\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Kink versus notch. Top row: a kink raises the marginal rate at $z^*$ without a discontinuity in net income, so taxpayers bunch just below $z^*$ and the upper branch of the budget set starts at $z^*$. Bottom row: a notch lowers net income by a fixed amount $T$ at $z^*$, which makes the interval $(z^*,\tmspace  +\thickmuskip {.2777em} z^*+T)$ dominated; its mass piles up at $z^*$ and the interval is left empty. Simulated with a quasilinear economy and uniformly distributed ability; income in million CLP.}}{2}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:kink_notch}{{1}{2}{Kink versus notch. Top row: a kink raises the marginal rate at $z^*$ without a discontinuity in net income, so taxpayers bunch just below $z^*$ and the upper branch of the budget set starts at $z^*$. Bottom row: a notch lowers net income by a fixed amount $T$ at $z^*$, which makes the interval $(z^*,\; z^*+T)$ dominated; its mass piles up at $z^*$ and the interval is left empty. Simulated with a quasilinear economy and uniformly distributed ability; income in million CLP}{figure.1}{}}
